        "text": "PA) Oral Communication\n\n1. Learning the Sounds\n\n's' and 'z' Sound in Plurals\n\ni. Read and say the given plural nouns.\n\n/s/ sound\n\n/z/ sound\n\nrabbit\n\nrabbits\n\nbed\n\nbeds\n\ncup\n\ncups\n\nsparrow sparrows\n\nduck\n\nducks\n\nroom\n\nrooms\n\nRead Me\n\nSome plural nouns end with /s/ sound, for example books, cups, cats, etc. Some plural nouns end with Izl sound, for\n\nbat\n\nbats\n\nhare\n\nhares\n\nexample trees, cars,\n\netc.\n\nfrock\n\nfrocks\n\nword\n\nwords\n\nii. Circle (0) the plurals that end in /s/ sound and square ()\n\nthe plurals that end in /z/ sound.\n\nants\n\npandas\n\nhat\n\nsnakes\n\ntigers\n\nbooks\n\nflowers\n\ndogs\n\nT A 1 (i) Tell students how to recognise /s/ or /z/ sound. When the singular ends with an unvoiced sound (no vibration in the larynx), the plural is pronounced /s/. When the singular ends with a voiced sound (vibration in the larynx), the plural is pronounced /z/. Encourage them to share more plurals ending with an/s/ and /zi sound.\n\n2. Learning to Speak\n\ni. Read and practise the given dialogue.\n\nHare: Tortoise:\n\nHare: Tortoise:\n\nHare:\n\nTortoise:\n\nHello, little Tortoise! How are you? Hi, I am fine. How are you?\n\nI am also fine. Can we play together? Yes, sure. What do you want to play?\n\nLet's have a race. Do you like racing? Yes, I really like it. Let's race.\n\nii. Read the given dialogue and express your enjoyment while\n\nplaying in your school.\n\nB) Reading and Critical Thinking",
